# ===== A TOUR of the rev language and the debugger. Press Enter to run each step; lines starting with # are narration. =====
# Run it:  npm run rvm -- debug examples/rev/tour.rev --demo examples/rev/tour.dbg   (open examples/rev/tour.rev beside it)
#
# The program has not started yet. "where" shows the time and the line about to run.
where
# A breakpoint on line 13, the first statement of main, then "c" (continue) runs up to it.
break 13
c
# "s" runs the line you are standing on and stops at the next one. Line 13 is: total += a + b
s
# "p" prints the variables. total is 9. Nothing was overwritten: it was updated in place.
p
# Line 14 is an if with no fi (its test survives the branches). It prints "big" to the console.
s
console
# Line 15 swaps a and b when they are out of order, and sets a flag f. That flag is GARBAGE: the price of the swap.
s
p
# THE POINT OF THE PROJECT. Who last wrote f? Run time BACKWARDS until it changes.
rwatch f
# We are just before f += 1 ran: f is back to 0 (the swap on the same line has happened, the flag has not). No log was kept.
p
# "b" steps back a whole source line. The swap undoes, and so does the "big" that line 14 printed.
b
p
console
# Time is a dial. Run forward to the end...
c
console
p
# How much history did the machine keep for all of that?
history
# ...and now backwards ("rc" = reverse continue). It stops at our breakpoint on line 13, and the printed text has un-printed.
rc
console
# Zero bytes. That is the idea; the rest of the project is about what it costs. Now try commands yourself: help lists them, quit leaves.
